[16/19] Interpretation lock...
1. TEST126 asks only whether better intervention directions exist around the locked semantic seed at fixed L18 and fixed last-token position.
2. TEST126 does not test whether a runtime navigator can identify those directions.
3. Candidates inside each angular shell have the same designed cosine distance from the semantic seed, preventing the TEST123/124 angle confound from explaining within-shell differences.
4. The 1000-way oracle maximum is treated only as an upper bound because searching many candidates creates winner's-curse / multiple-search optimism.
5. The top-decile analysis is therefore used as a distributed directional-opportunity diagnostic rather than relying only on the single best candidate.
6. The same relative intervention magnitude is requested for the seed and all candidates; realized BF16 magnitude and direction are audited.
7. L18 is intentionally fixed because TEST125-A found L18 was the best tested source for L19 on all 40 TEST125-A prompts; TEST126 is not another layer-selection assay.
8. A positive TEST126 result would establish only that the locked semantic representation direction is not necessarily the best intervention direction in the tested local neighborhood.
9. Context dependence is NOT established by TEST126. TEST127 must use new prompts to distinguish prompt-specific directional opportunity from a globally superior fixed candidate.
10. No DRA controller, Mercek, Jacobian, gradient, weight update, manifold recovery, pseudo-training, physical-energy, behavioral-efficacy, or semantic-causality claim is made.
11. All TEST126 prompts become exposed after this run and must not be reused as blind TEST127 prompts.

